WEBVTT
Language: en

NOTE
Working session of the Concordance project, recorded and transcribed for the wiki.
The names below are those of the fictional participants of the demonstration corpus;
the build replaces them by stable pseudonyms before anything is published.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:44.000
<v Anna Duval>The subject today is the twin resources. A workshop exists as a deck, as notes and as a transcript, and the reader must see one page.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:37.700
<v Dario Mensah>The first import of a corpus puts every file in one commit. If the commit alone grouped files, the whole repository would be one document.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:05.400
<v Bram Kessler>That is why the commit and the folder only reinforce a pair. A pair is founded by a declaration in the frontmatter, a shared base name, a title equal to a heading, or similar text.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:19.100
<v Anna Duval>Similar text is the expensive part. Comparing every pair of a corpus of a thousand files is half a million comparisons.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:21.800
<v Bram Kessler>Hence the proposal: each text goes to its comparison form, then to shingles of five words, then to a minhash signature of one hundred and twenty-eight functions under a fixed seed. Banding enumerates the candidate pairs; two signatures that share no band are never compared.

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:19.500
<v Dario Mensah>And the estimate is accurate to about one tenth. Is that enough to merge two files without a human looking?

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:43.200
<v Bram Kessler>In auto mode the pairs estimated at one half or more get their exact jaccard index recomputed on the full shingle sets. The finding gives the share of lines in common.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:20.900
<v Anna Duval>A deck and its notes rarely share sentences, though. The deck says three words per slide; the notes say a paragraph per slide.

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:19.600
<v Bram Kessler>Then the pair is an inclusion rather than a duplicate: when the word counts differ by more than half, the content signal is capped, and the finding says so. The base name and the title do the rest.

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:27.300
<v Dario Mensah>What does the reader see on the page? I want the reason the files were grouped, not only the tabs.

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:55.000
<v Anna Duval>The panel names the signals of every scored pair: same folder, same base name, same commit, high textual overlap. And the lock file wins over the score, both ways.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:43.700
<v Dario Mensah>Then the determinism question. Two runs, or a shuffled input, must give the same groups, or the build log is unreadable from one night to the next.

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:41.400
<v Bram Kessler>Resources are sorted by identifier before anything else, the seed is fixed and every output is sorted. The golden corpus has a test for it.

00:57:55.000 --> 00:58:04.100
<v Anna Duval>Decision of the session, then: minhash for twin resources, as Bram Kessler described it, with the exact index recomputed on the close pairs. Dario Mensah reviews the finding wording.
